</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Architectural Compatibility Is the Key</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having localized the failure to the attention pathway, one question remains: what determines whether transferred attention is functional across teacher families?
We address this below, starting with a simple native-architecture rescue.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Native-Architecture Rescue</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> suggest that transferring <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only</span> the attention can recover the full benefit of the whole teacher’s pre-trained weights under a standard teacher-student architecture.
However, we revisit this claim in <a href="#S3" title="3 Attention Transfer Is Not Universally Effective ‣ Attention Transfer Is Not Universally Effective for Vision Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> to find that this does not hold universally, and systematically localize the failure to the attention pathway in <a href="#S4" title="4 Localizing the Failure ‣ Attention Transfer Is Not Universally Effective for Vision Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, further confirming that this failure is broadly distributed across attention layers and Q/K/V terms.
This leads us to hypothesize that the missing factor is <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">architectural compatibility</em> in the default protocol: transferred attention remains usable only when the student preserves the architectural components under which the teacher originally learned its attention patterns.
Concretely, each failure family in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Attention Transfer Is Not Universally Effective for Vision Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> contains one or more such components that are absent from the standard student architecture: <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LayerScale<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">3</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote3.10" class="ltx_text">LayerScale</span><span id="footnote3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> was originally introduced in CaiT </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="footnote3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">[</span><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a><span id="footnote3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">]</span></cite><span id="footnote3.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> to stabilize the training of very deep ViTs, and is not adopted by the ViT implementations of </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="footnote3.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">[</span><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a><span id="footnote3.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">]</span></cite><span id="footnote3.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">, which is used in the standard teacher-student architecture.</span></span></span></span></span> in DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and DINOv2-wr <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PreLayerNorm</span> in CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RelativePositionBias</span> in BEiTv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
These components from the native architecture define the scaling, normalization, and positional context under which the teacher’s attention was learned.
Without them, transferring attention into the standard student may instantiate the teacher’s routing patterns through a mismatched computation pathway.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:47%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span><span id="S5.T5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">No-Transfer baseline comparison.</span>
We compare from-scratch No-Transfer baselines between standard student and native-arch student.
The native-arch baseline is lower than the standard-arch one, confirming the rescue is not driven by the added components’ own capacity.
</figcaption>
